From 626c7b2501f5414e5a1f62d30f54ff53694061e1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexios Lyrakis <alexios.lyrakis@amd.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 11:43:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [AMDGPU] Rematerialize invariant LDS loads instead of
 spilling them

When a value loaded from LDS with !invariant.load is live across a region
of high register pressure (e.g. an MFMA operand fragment kept live across
a loop), the register allocator spills it to scratch and reloads it on the
critical path. Re-reading it from LDS is much cheaper, and legal because the
memory does not change. Three pieces enable this:

* canRemat / isReMaterializableImpl accept plain DS loads (no store/atomic,
  no GDS, no M0 read) whose memory operands are all invariant LDS loads,
  and isIgnorableUse treats their implicit EXEC use like a VALU's (the value
  only matters in lanes active at the use).

* Wide MFMA operands are usually built from two ds_read_b128 feeding a
  256-bit REG_SEQUENCE, which the allocator cannot rematerialize (it only
  re-issues single-instruction definitions). The new pre-RA SSA pass
  SIFormInvariantLDSReadPairs combines such pairs (same address register,
  invariant, loads have no other uses) into DS_READ_B256_INVARIANT_PSEUDO,
  a single rematerializable definition.

* expandPostRAPseudo splits the pseudo back into the two ds_read_b128,
  loading the high half first if the (killed) address register was
  allocated inside the low half of the destination.

-amdgpu-remat-invariant-lds-loads and -amdgpu-form-invariant-lds-read-pairs
turn the two parts off.

On an fp8 FMHA prefill kernel (gfx950, hd256) whose Q fragment was spilled
inside the main loop, the scratch reload in the loop becomes an LDS re-read
(scratch 44 -> 12 bytes) and the kernel runs ~8% faster.

The pass is registered for the legacy pass manager only.
